@SamuelPurvine @lamccue

The `AudioMediaPlayer` destructor has an issue where it effectively destroys the player's conference port twice, in a thread-unsafe way that leaves an opportunity for another thread to see a conference port used by a player as free-for-use, and reserve it for other purposes before ultimately being destroyed again, causing crashes.

We see this race condition ~1 in 5 times when attempting to disrupt an ongoing call with a talkdown call, where `StopTransmit()` is interrupted by the PJSIP thread answering the incoming call.

Here's an example of how this can happen: 
```
AudioMediaPlayer::~AudioMediaPlayer()
{
    if (playerId != PJSUA_INVALID_ID) {
        // Example: media player is using slot 2, aka pjsua_var.player[playerId].slot == 2

        unregisterMediaPort(); <----- calls pjsua_conf_remove_port() which removes slot 2, but does not de-init/clear pjsua_var.player[playerId].slot

        // INCOMING CALL THREAD OCCURS HERE - finds any empty conf port and adds a new port for the incoming call, sees slot 2 is free and sets up port there

        pjsua_player_destroy(playerId); <----- also calls pjsua_conf_remove_port() on pjsua_var.player[playerId].slot, which still == 2
        
        // This removes the now-active talkdown port, program crashes on talkdown connect audio
    }
}
```

The approach taken in this PR to fix this problem is taken from a pattern used elsewhere in media.cpp presumably to prevent this exact problem - invalidating the `AudioMedia` `id` such that `unregisterMediaPort()` does not attempt to remove the port unnecessarily: https://github.com/verkada/pjproject/blob/7eec09d2f1ab6b40d7b447d40c62c47e53880e44/pjsip/src/pjsua2/media.cpp#L665-L670

Relevant segment of `unregisterMediaPort()`: https://github.com/verkada/pjproject/blob/7eec09d2f1ab6b40d7b447d40c62c47e53880e44/pjsip/src/pjsua2/media.cpp#L177-L182

This ensures that the only attempt to remove an `AudioMediaPlayer`'s port occurs in `pjsua_player_destroy()`, therefore guaranteeing that `pjsua_player_destroy()` does not delete a now-occupied port mistakenly.

# Adding `libsql`

This pull request adds `libsql` and all the related components it needs to run:

- A primary database instance
- A replica database instance
- An nginx container to proxy HTTP and gRPC requests to the `libsql` replica database

> Note: All requests through the `db_proxy` (nginx), go to the replica instance. I'm no database architect so I don't know the proper way to do this. However, it should suffice for what we are doing

## Interacting with the DB

- [HTTP API docs](https://github.com/tursodatabase/libsql/blob/main/docs/http_api.md) - Requests made from the TDM to the database using this method should send requests to `db_proxy:8080` as this is the internal network mapping within the docker-compose stack to reach to database proxy (nginx) container. (`http:db_proxy:8080` also works - with the http prefix)
- [libsql-js](https://github.com/tursodatabase/libsql-js) javascript client library - I already installed the required packages and dependencies to use this library in this PR so it should be good to go! Probably better to use this than the HTTP API anyways

Check out the [`examples`](https://github.com/the-hideout/tarkov-data-manager/tree/libsql/src/tarkov-data-manager/examples) directory to see some examples on how you can `read` and `write` things to the database

Read the [database](https://github.com/the-hideout/tarkov-data-manager/blob/libsql/docs/database.md) docs to learn more as well

## Example

Here is an example using the `libsql-js` node package to `write` and `read` from the `libsql` database:

```js
// after starting up the docker-compose stack with 'make run' exec onto this container and run:
// node examples/write_to_db.js

const Database = require('libsql');

const url = process.env.LIBSQL_URL;
const authToken = process.env.LIBSQL_AUTH_TOKEN; // not used currently

const opts = {
  authToken: authToken, // not used
};

const db = new Database(url, opts);

db.exec("CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, email TEXT)");
db.exec("INSERT INTO users (id, name, email) VALUES (1, 'Alice', 'alice@example.org')");
db.exec("INSERT INTO users (id, name, email) VALUES (2, 'Bob', 'bob@example.com')");

const row = db.prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?").get(1);

console.log(`Name: ${row.name}, email: ${row.email}`);

db.close();
process.exit(0);
```

> Note: I ran `make run` then opened a shell on the TDM container, then ran `node example/write_to_db.js` and this worked. It updated the local database volume files in the `data/libsql_data/` directory as well 🎉 

## AWS S3

The `libsql` instance is configured to do incremental backups to S3. If you have the correct access keys set for AWS S3, and no db configured, it will automatically pull the latest backup from S3 and then begin using it and contributing to the state in AWS S3.

At this time of writing, our bucket is currently empty as I haven't done any migration with the db stuffs:

<img width="1437" alt="Screenshot 2024-03-08 at 6 16 27 PM" src="https://github.com/the-hideout/tarkov-data-manager/assets/23362539/fd7e20a1-025c-424d-9ab3-d47000167f13">

Now the only issue I could potentially see with this is after we get the db populated with data and deployed. If we were to run a local instance that breaks the database, it might try to sync our local dev instance with the production back up (if credentials are set), and then blow up our production database. I'm not sure if this is the case but that sounds bad. Also, I'm not sure how `libsql` would work if multiple instances are running and all trying to sync to the AWS S3 bucket at once.

We don't have any plans on running multiple instances because we are poor open source devs so I don't see that being an issue but just a thought.
